{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JtYiV4mJ93TCABAw9ugeU7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations\n3\tdescription: \"lbuild-impl story-orchestrate ALWAYS terminates needs-ruling when the implementor surfaces a spec deviation; resolve by impl-lead direct acceptance, never loop resume.\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\tIn the lbuild-impl / lspec-core `ls-impl` runtime, when a story implementor surfaces ANY spec deviation, `buildStoryLeadFinalPackage` (lspec-core `dist/bin/lbuild-impl.js`, ~L13567) re-injects each implementor-surfaced deviation into the final package with `approvalStatus: \"needs-ruling\"` on EVERY package build, unconditionally. A caller ruling delivered via `story-orchestrate resume --ruling-file` IS durably recorded (callerInputHistory.rulings + acceptedRulingArtifact) but does NOT mutate those per-item approvalStatus values; `reverifyRulingRequest` re-derives the ruling request purely from items still marked `needs-ruling`, and `resolvedOutcome` then forces an `accept-story` planner action down to `needs-ruling`. Net effect: `story-orchestrate run`/`resume` ALWAYS terminates `needs-ruling` for a story whose implementor surfaced a deviation. The story-lead loop structurally cannot self-clear it.\n11\t\n12\t**Why:** By design — acceptance is impl-lead authority; the CLI/story-lead never accepts a story or self-approves a deviation. The `needs-ruling` terminal is the loop correctly handing the deviation decision to the orchestrator (me).\n13\t\n14\t**How to apply:** Do NOT loop `story-orchestrate resume` hoping for an `accepted` terminal — it will return `needs-ruling` forever and waste provider turns/tokens. Instead: (1) evaluate the deviation from evidence (verify any factual claims it makes); (2) deliver the ruling once via `resume --ruling-file` as the durable decision record — JSON schema is `.strict()` `{ rulingRequestId, decision, rationale, source }` (decision ∈ the request's allowedResponses, e.g. \"approve\"/\"reject\"); (3) then ACCEPT THE STORY DIRECTLY as impl-lead: run the recorded story gate yourself, write the complete receipt in `team-impl-log.md` (disposition the deviation as accepted-risk or fixed), and land the story commit. That sequence IS acceptance per the ls-impl operating model — a `needs-ruling` story-orchestrate terminal is not a blocker to impl-lead acceptance. Step (2)'s ruling can even be skipped if you record the decision directly in the receipt, but delivering it leaves a clean durable artifact. See [[pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build]].\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc8b0d70-702e-4abc-9faa-dda18cc81eaf","uuid":"4b91d0bd-d7ca-44bd-b538-c2d192697644","timestamp":"2026-06-14T05:23:34.596Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations.md","content":"---\nname: lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations\ndescription: \"lbuild-impl story-orchestrate ALWAYS terminates needs-ruling when the implementor surfaces a spec deviation; resolve by impl-lead direct acceptance, never loop resume.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\n---\n\nIn the lbuild-impl / lspec-core `ls-impl` runtime, when a story implementor surfaces ANY spec deviation, `buildStoryLeadFinalPackage` (lspec-core `dist/bin/lbuild-impl.js`, ~L13567) re-injects each implementor-surfaced deviation into the final package with `approvalStatus: \"needs-ruling\"` on EVERY package build, unconditionally. A caller ruling delivered via `story-orchestrate resume --ruling-file` IS durably recorded (callerInputHistory.rulings + acceptedRulingArtifact) but does NOT mutate those per-item approvalStatus values; `reverifyRulingRequest` re-derives the ruling request purely from items still marked `needs-ruling`, and `resolvedOutcome` then forces an `accept-story` planner action down to `needs-ruling`. Net effect: `story-orchestrate run`/`resume` ALWAYS terminates `needs-ruling` for a story whose implementor surfaced a deviation. The story-lead loop structurally cannot self-clear it.\n\n**Why:** By design — acceptance is impl-lead authority; the CLI/story-lead never accepts a story or self-approves a deviation. The `needs-ruling` terminal is the loop correctly handing the deviation decision to the orchestrator (me).\n\n**How to apply:** Do NOT loop `story-orchestrate resume` hoping for an `accepted` terminal — it will return `needs-ruling` forever and waste provider turns/tokens. Instead: (1) evaluate the deviation from evidence (verify any factual claims it makes); (2) deliver the ruling once via `resume --ruling-file` as the durable decision record — JSON schema is `.strict()` `{ rulingRequestId, decision, rationale, source }` (decision ∈ the request's allowedResponses, e.g. \"approve\"/\"reject\"); (3) then ACCEPT THE STORY DIRECTLY as impl-lead: run the recorded story gate yourself, write the complete receipt in `team-impl-log.md` (disposition the deviation as accepted-risk or fixed), and land the story commit. That sequence IS acceptance per the ls-impl operating model — a `needs-ruling` story-orchestrate terminal is not a blocker to impl-lead acceptance. Step (2)'s ruling can even be skipped if you record the decision directly in the receipt, but delivering it leaves a clean durable artifact. See [[pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build]].\n","numLines":15,"startLine":1,"totalLines":15}}}
